      "text": "We can use the Pythagorean theorem to solve this problem. We can split the prism into one of the faces and draw triangles on each of the 3 sides. Let's use the 10-inch side on the top and the 20-inch side on the right side of the prism.\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the length of the diagonal formed by the top and right side of the prism. We split the diagonal into 2 right triangles, so we can use Pythagoras to find the length of the diagonal each time.\n\nThe top-right triangle's sides are 10 inches, 10 inches, and the diagonal connecting points A and B. Plugging these values into the Pythagorean theorem, we get:\n\n$$d^2 = 10^2 + 10^2 = 200$$\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\n$$d = \\sqrt{200} = \\sqrt{10^2 \\cdot 2} = 10 \\sqrt{2}$$\n\nSo, the length of the diagonal connecting points A and B is $\\boxed{10\\sqrt{2}}$ inches. The answer is: 10\\sqrt{2}",
