      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the length of the diagonal connecting points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) in the rectangular prism, we can use the three-dimensional version of the Pythagorean theorem. The three-dimensional Pythagorean theorem states that for a rectangular prism with side lengths \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\), the length of the diagonal \\(d\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2 + c^2}\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, the side lengths of the rectangular prism are:\n- \\(a = 10\\) inches\n- \\(b = 20\\) inches\n- \\(c = 10\\) inches\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{10^2 + 20^2 + 10^2}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the squares of each side length:\n\n\\[\n10^2 = 100\n\\]\n\\[\n20^2 = 400\n\\]\n\\[\n10^2 = 100\n\\]\n\nNext, we sum these squares:\n\n\\[\n100 + 400 + 100 = 600\n\\]\n\nFinally, we take the square root of the sum:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{600}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\sqrt{600}\\), we factorize 600:\n\n\\[\n600 = 6 \\times 100 = 6 \\times 10^2\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{600} = \\sqrt{6 \\times 10^2} = \\sqrt{6} \\times \\sqrt{10^2} = \\sqrt{6} \\times 10 = 10\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the diagonal connecting points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10\\sqrt{6}}\n\\]",
