      "text": "We can solve this problem by utilizing the three-dimensional version of the Pythagorean theorem. To solve this, we consider what the formula $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}$ would mean for dimensions $x$, $y$, and $z$. \n\nFirst, recall that we have a rectangular prism that measures 10 inches in length, 20 inches in width, and 10 inches in height. Since we are looking for the length of the diagonal connecting point $A$ (located at the bottom-left-front corner of the prism) to point $B$ (located at the top-right-back corner of the prism), the diagonal $d$ can be calculated using the length of the prism as base and height and the height itself as the diagonal to be found.\n\nWe can set up the formula for the diagonal as follows:\n\\begin{align*}\nd &= \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\n\\end{align*}\nwhere $x = 10$ inches (length), $y = 10$ inches (width), and $z = 20$ inches (height). Substituting in the given dimensions, we have:\n\\begin{align*}\nd &= \\sqrt{10^2 + 10^2 + 20^2} \\\\\n  &= \\sqrt{100 + 100 + 400} \\\\\n  &= \\sqrt{600} \\\\\n  &= \\sqrt{100 \\cdot 6} \\\\\n  &= 10\\sqrt{6}\n\\end{align*}\nTherefore, the length of the diagonal connecting point $A$ and point $B$ is $\\boxed{10\\sqrt{6}}$ inches.",
